  "raw": {
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Milwaukee-WI/Congregate-Care--Relative--Adoptive--and-Foster-Parent--CRAFT--Training-Manager_JR10014711": {
      "title": "Congregate Care, Relative, Adoptive, and Foster Parent (CRAFT) Training Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Congregate Care, Relative, Adoptive, and Foster Parent (CRAFT) Training Manager Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Outreach Program Manager Job Duties: This position manages a key program of the Wisconsin Child Welfare Partnership for Professional Development-UW Milwaukee (WCWPDS-UWM) that is housed in the Dept. of Social Work in the Helen Bader School of Social Welfare. Its main function is to manage training and professional development for Congregate Care Staff, Relative, Adoptive, and Foster Parents throughout Wisconsin.  This is a highly visible position with significant responsibility for team leadership, instruction, program and curriculum planning, and management of statewide partner relationships.    Key Job Responsibilities: Key Responsibilities include, but are not limited to:     35%         Plan and oversee professional development/training offerings     1.       Oversee annual professional development calendar, including trainers and locations as needed   2.       Oversee marketing/communication    3.       Oversee evaluation processes   4.       Develop and lead projects/new curriculum      30%         Develop and oversee funder, customer, and inter-state collaborations    1.       Develop and enhance partnerships with array of customer groups    2.       Lead and participate in statewide practice and strategic initiatives   3.       Oversee communication, reporting and project/budget planning with DCF and other funders       20%         Supervise staff     1.       Coaching and develop CRAFT team   2.       Lead staff meetings         15%         Participate on WCWPDS-UWM management team    1.       Collaborate with other WCWPDS management staff in creating and managing program procedures and solving problems as needed   2.       Collaborate with other WCWPDS programs on cross-program initiatives/offerings    3.       Lead/participate in management team development activities   Lead/participate on management team subcommittees as needed      Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited college/university in adult education, child development, family systems, child/family policy, social work or other field directly related to the responsibilities of the position  Professional experience with adult education, curriculum development, learning evaluation and/or other aspects of professional development   Preferred Qualifications: Experience serving children, youth and/or families in home or out-of-home setting   Knowledge of early childhood development, child abuse and neglect, parenting practices, WI state licensing and training requirements for alternate caregivers    Experience hiring, training and supervising professional staff   Target Salary Range: The target hiring range to $79,000 to $90,000 annually. Final salary offer will be determined based on qualifications and experience. How to Apply: Applicants are required to apply online and will be expected to provide:  A cover letter that addresses the candidate’s professional level work experience as it relates to all required and preferred qualifications.  A resume  Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach all required documents prior to submitting the online application to ensure full consideration. Additional documents submitted aside from a cover letter and resume will not be considered.   In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates' application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications.  Finalist candidate(s) being considered for hire will be required to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified.  Apply electronically by the application deadline date of September 24, 2026 . Questions about this position should be directed to Trevor Maloney at maloney3@uwm.edu .  Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Milwaukee-WI/Director-of-Financial-Aid_JR10014558": {
      "title": "Director of Financial Aid",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Director of Financial Aid Job Category: Limited Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Financial Aid Director (Inst) Job Duties: The Director of Financial Aid provides strategic and operational leadership for the Office of Financial Aid Administration (OFAA) and is responsible for the administration, compliance, and delivery of federal, state, and select institutional financial aid programs. The Director serves as the University's principal authority on Title IV and state student financial aid regulations and related compliance matters, ensuring the institution maintains full compliance with all applicable federal and state laws, regulations, reporting requirements, and audit standards.   This position reports to the Chief Enrollment Operations Officer and Executive Director of Student Financial Services (SFS). SFS at UWM is comprised of OFAA , the Office of Student Accounts (OSA) , the Office of Student Scholarships (OSS) , and the Student Financial Service Center (SFSC) . The Director collaborates regularly with the Executive Director and other SFS department leaders to advance a coordinated and strategic approach to supporting student finances . Daily student advising and other outreach and support are provided by dedicated advisors in the SFSC.   As a key member of the Division of Strategic Enrollment Management and Student Success, the Director provides leadership that supports student access, persistence, and completion through efficient, compliant, and student-centered financial aid operations. The Director is responsible for establishing and maintaining a culture of compliance, continuous improvement, operational excellence, accountability, and collaboration while utilizing technology and data-informed decision making to improve service delivery and institutional effectiveness.   T his role offers flexibility for a blended in-office and remote schedule over time . The successful candidate will be expected to report to campus multiple days per week.   Major Responsibilities: 40% Administration and Management   The Director of Financial Aid has overall responsibility for directing the administration and management of OFAA. Provides leadership for strategic planning, organizational effectiveness, personnel management, resource allocation, technology utilization , business process improvement, customer service, and operational performance. Directs development, implementation, and assessment of policies and procedures supporting the delivery of governmental financial aid programs. Leads departmental planning efforts aligned with university enrollment goals and student success priorities. Maintains effective staffing levels and develops a positive, collaborative, and accountable work environment. Responsible for hiring, supervising, coaching, evaluating, and developing professional staff.   30% Regulatory Compliance, Policy Development and Risk Management   Serves as UWM’s institutional subject matter expert on Title IV student financial aid regulations and state financial aid requirements. Interprets and operationalizes federal statutes, regulations, Dear Colleague Letters, Electronic Announcements, Federal Student Aid Handbook guidance, and state directives. Develops and maintains policies, procedures, internal controls, and compliance monitoring practices. Directs institutional preparation for audits, reviews, and federal program reviews. Oversees corrective action activities, identifies compliance risks, and advises university leadership regarding regulatory implications and opportunities to mitigate institutional liability.   15% Federal and State Aid Program Administration and Reporting   Provides direct oversight of federal , state , and select institutional financial aid programs, including Pell Grants, Direct Loans, Federal Work Study, Federal Supplemental Educational Opportunity Grant ( FSEOG ) , Teacher Education Assistance for College and Higher Education ( TEACH ) Grants, Wisconsin Grant programs, the Milwaukee Tuition Promise program, military and veteran educational benefits coordination, and other governmental aid programs. Oversees the Fiscal Operations Report and Application to Participate ( FISAP ) preparation, reconciliation activities, reporting requirements, fund stewardship, and compliance submissions. Ensures timely awarding, disbursement, reconciliation, and administration of aid programs while maximizing operational effectiveness.   10% Leadership, Collaboration, and External Relations   Establishes and maintains effective working relationships with university leadership, Strategic Enrollment Management and Student Success departments , academic departments, the Universities of Wisconsin System, state agencies, and federal partners. Represents the university in system-wide, state, regional, and national financial aid organizations and discussions. Advises institutional leaders regarding financial aid regulatory changes, compliance implications, and operational best practices. Collaborates with campus partners to support enrollment, retention, affordability, and student success initiatives.   5% Other   Other duties as assigned by university/ divisional leadership.   Key Job Responsibilities: Exercises supervisory authority, including hiring, transferring, suspending, promoting, managing conduct and performance, discharging, assigning, rewarding, disciplining, and/or approving hours worked of at least 2.0 full-time equivalent (FTE) employees Develops operating policies and procedures to comply with regulations, institutional policies, and unit objectives Serves as the unit liaison to internal and external stakeholder groups providing organizational information and representing the interests of the unit Develops and audits the unit budget Advises institutional leadership and unit leadership on financial aid matters Directs strategic planning initiatives and establishes unit objectives to ensure appropriate use of resources and alignment with the strategic plan Department: This is an exciting opportunity to join a student-centered institution committed to access, affordability, and student success while leading a talented, veteran team responsible for the administration of federal, state, institutional, and private financial aid programs. As Wisconsin's premier urban research university, UWM serves more than 23,000 students and is a national leader in providing educational opportunity and social mobility. The Director of Financial Aid will play a critical leadership role within Student Financial Services and the Division of Strategic Enrollment Management & Student Success, helping to shape institutional strategy while building upon the strong foundation of an experienced and highly respected staff recognized for its professionalism, dedication, and student-centered approach. The primary responsibility of the Office of Financial Aid Administration is determining a student’s eligibility for financial aid and veteran related funds while at the same time remaining in compliance with the various federal, state, UW System, and institutional regulations and guidance. We seek a director who will continue the tradition of excellence that has long defined financial aid administration at UWM. Compensation: $115,000-$130,000 (Final salary offer will be determined based on qualifications and experience). This position also offers an excellent benefits package, including health insurance, paid sick leave, personal, vacation and legal holiday time off as well as contribution towards the cost of several other employee benefits, including a pension. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree or higher       At least seven (7) years of progressively responsible financial aid experience, including at least three years of supervisory or leadership experience       Demonstrated advanced knowledge of federal Title IV regulations, state financial aid programs, and related compliance requirements       Demonstrated ability to interpret and implement complex regulatory guidance while effectively managing compliance and institutional risk   Experience with federal financial aid systems and reporting tools including the FISAP, Common Origination & Disbursement (COD) system, National Student Loan Data System (NSLDS), and the Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA) Partner Portal Preferred Qualifications: Master's degree in higher education administration or a related field, or more than 15 years working professionally in higher education   Experience administering financial aid at a large public university with a broad range of student populations and financial aid needs, including first-generation students and Pell Grant recipients    Demonstrated strong leadership, analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills, with a collaborative and student-centered approach       Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the proven ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines in a fast-paced environment       Certified Financial Aid Administrator (FAAC) designation or other advanced financial aid certification    Experience leading financial aid audits, federal program reviews, or other compliance-related initiatives       Familiarity with financial aid technology solutions, workflow automation, and reporting tools       Knowledge of Universities of Wisconsin policies and procedures and/or usage of PeopleSoft Campus Solutions       Active participation in professional financial aid associations Physical Requirements: Sitting: Work at a desk for many hours each day.   Viewing: Look at computer monitors to read data and reports.   Speaking: Talk clearly during meeting s and staff training .   Listening: Hear and understand questions from students and staff.   Hand use: Type on a keyboard and use a mouse.   Walking: Move across campus for meetings with other leaders.   Carrying: Bring light items like folders, laptops, or papers to events.   Travel: Drive or fly to occasional off-site professional conferences.   How to Apply: Interested applicants are required to apply online and provide a cover letter and resume  addressing your professional level work experience as it relates to all required and preferred qualifications. Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach all required documents prior to submitting the online application. Applications missing any of the required documents listed prior to the application deadline will be marked as incomplete and may not be considered.  In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates' application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications. Additional documents submitted aside from a   cover letter and resume   will not be reviewed.      Finalist candidates(s) will be asked to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified.     Apply electronically by the application deadline date of Sunday, October 11th, 2026, at 11:59pm.  Questions about this position should be directed to  Coua Xiong at xion2447@uwm.edu.   Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Milwaukee-WI/Medical-Office-Assistant--SHAW_JR10014679": {
      "title": "Medical Office Assistant, SHAW",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Medical Office Assistant, SHAW Job Category: University Staff Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Medical Office Assistant Job Duties: The Medical Office Assistant in Patient Support Services at UWM’s Student Health and Wellness Center provides administrative and customer service support in a healthcare setting. This position helps ensure smooth daily operations while delivering high-quality service to patients and staff. Key Job Responsibilities: Provides day-to-day operational support to medical work unit personnel    Prepares documents for patient admissions and appointments    Serves as front desk staff within a healthcare facility or clinic    Greets patients, schedules appointments, maintains calendars, schedules tests, and makes referrals according to instructions    Receives, submits, and responds to inquiries according to established policies and procedures  Department: The Student Health and Wellness Center is a recognized and accredited leader in student health providing excellent health care in partnership with the campus community. The mission of the Student Health and Wellness Center is to assist students in identifying, understanding and responding to current health issues through medical and mental health care and health promotion programs; and to assist the UWM campus community through collaborative partnerships designed to enhance the university’s academic and community service mission.   Compensation: Starting salary between $18.00-$20.00/hour (Final offer will be determined by experience, education and qualifications at the time of hire). This position also offers an excellent benefit package, including health insurance, paid sick leave, personal, vacation and legal holiday time off as well as contribution towards the cost of several other employee benefits, including a pension.  Required Qualifications: High School Diploma or GED   Minimum of 6 months of experience working in a customer/client-facing support role within an office environment   Preferred Qualifications: Experience using electronic health record system(s)   Experience in customer service with problem solving situations both in person and over the phone   Experience working with a young adult, underserved, diverse population within a fast paced, constantly changing environment    Demonstrated experience obtaining information while protecting and maintaining patient confidentiality   Demonstrated basic knowledge of the work within a healthcare office environment   Demonstrated ability receiving/handling money, crediting patient accounts electronically, balancing monies at end of shift   Physical Requirements:  Ability to remain in a stationary position (sitting and/or standing) for extended periods while performing administrative, patient service, or computer-based work.   Ability to frequently move throughout a clinic or office to greet patients, escort patients, deliver materials, or retrieve supplies.   Ability to operate a computer, keyboard, mouse, telephone, copier, scanner, and other standard office equipment for prolonged periods.   Ability to read printed materials and electronic medical records, and accurately enter information into computer systems.   Ability to occasionally bend, stoop, reach, kneel, or squat to retrieve files, office supplies, or equipment.   Ability to occasionally lift, carry, push, or pull office supplies, records, or equipment weighing up to 10–20 pounds.   How to Apply: Interested applicants are required to  apply online and  provide a  cover  letter  and  resume  addressing your professional level work experience. Your cover letter and resume must address your qualifications, experience, and knowledge as it specifically relates to the minimum and preferred qualifications and why you are interested in applying for this position.   Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach all required documents prior to submitting the online application. Applications missing any of the required documents listed prior to the application deadline will be marked as incomplete and may not be considered.  In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates' application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications. Additional documents submitted aside from a cover letter and resume will not be reviewed.      Finalist candidates(s) will be asked to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified.  This is a continuous recruitment with an initial review date of Monday, September 14th, 2026. To ensure consideration, applications must be submitted by Mon day, September 14th, 2026, at 11:59pm.   Applications submitted after 09/14/2026 may not be reviewed.    Questions about this position should be directed to  Coua Xiong at xion2447@uwm.edu. Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
