    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Milwaukee-WI/Student-Connection---Achievement-Manager--Roberto-Hernandez-Center_JR10014519": {
      "title": "Student Connection & Achievement Manager, Roberto Hernandez Center",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Student Connection & Achievement Manager, Roberto Hernandez Center Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Student Affairs Manager Job Duties: What You Will Do: Reporting to the Assistant Vice Chancellor of Student Affairs, the Student Connection & Achievement Manager provides leadership for the daily operations, programs, services, and initiatives of the Roberto Hernández Center. The position advances student success by fostering engagement, belonging, leadership development, and academic achievement among Latino/a/e students and other students served by the Center. The Student Connection & Achievement Manager oversees the implementation and assessment of programs and services that support the Center's mission, supervises professional and student staff, manages fiscal and operational resources, and develops collaborative partnerships across campus and within the community.     Major Responsibilities:  STUDENT ENGAGEMENT, DEVELOPMENT, AND PROGRAMMING 25%     Develop, implement, and assess programs, services, and initiatives that promote student engagement, belonging, persistence, retention, and academic success.    Lead the planning and execution of signature cultural and heritage programs, welcome programs, educational workshops and leadership programs, and community-building initiatives that support the mission of the Roberto Hernández Center.    Facilitate opportunities for students to develop leadership skills, cultural awareness, personal growth, and community connections.    Advise and support student organizations and student leaders affiliated with the Center.    Collaborate with campus partners to support Welcome programs, orientation engagement, enrollment, retention, persistence, and graduation initiatives.    Assess participation, student learning outcomes, and program effectiveness to guide continuous improvement efforts.        LEADERSHIP AND SUPERVISION 25%     Provide leadership for the day-to-day operations of the Roberto Hernández Center.    Recruit, hire, train, supervise, develop, and evaluate professional staff, graduate assistants, student employees, and interns.    Foster a collaborative, student-centered, and team-oriented work environment.    Establish performance expectations and support professional development opportunities that enhance staff effectiveness and growth.    Delegate responsibilities and monitor progress toward departmental goals and objectives.     Support divisional initiatives and participate in committees, work groups, and special projects as assigned.        STRATEGIC PLANNING, ASSESSMENT, AND STUDENT SUCCESS OUTCOMES 20%     Develop and maintain assessment practices that evaluate program effectiveness, student engagement, and student success outcomes, identifying equity gaps affecting Latino/a/e students.    Monitor retention and persistence trends, and other relevant student success indicators.     Utilize assessment data to inform decision-making and improve services.     Develop action plans based on student success data.     Stay informed of emerging trends, best practices, and issues affecting Latino/a/e students    Recommend programmatic and operational improvements that support student success and align with divisional priorities.        ADMINISTRATIVE AND FISCAL MANAGEMENT 20%     Manage the daily operations of the Roberto Hernández Center.    Develop, administer, and monitor departmental budgets, including Segregated University Fee allocations, foundation accounts, grants, and other funding sources.    Ensure responsible stewardship of financial resources and compliance with institutional policies and procedures.     Maintain knowledge of university policies, procedures, and regulations relevant to departmental operations.    Prepare budget projections, expenditure analyses, and financial reports as needed.    Identify and pursue opportunities for supplemental funding and external partnerships that support departmental initiatives.         COLLABORATION, COMMUNITY, AND FAMILY ENGAGEMENT 10%     Develop and maintain relationships with local schools, community organizations, alumni, and families to strengthen pathways to enrollment, retention, and student success.    Serve as the Roberto Hernández Center liaison to internal and external partners.     Represent the Center at university and community events, meetings, and professional gatherings.    Other duties, as assigned by supervisor     Key Job Responsibilities: Serves as the program or unit liaison to internal and external stakeholder groups Schedules logistics and secures resources to support the operational plans of the program or unit Plans, organizes, administers, and manages processes and operations related to assigned area(s) of responsibility May monitor the program budget and approve expenditures Monitors, evaluates, and modifies processes, procedures, and/or standards, ensuring alignment with the Institution's mission, values, goals, and objectives, as well as all local, state, and federal laws and regulations. Advises and enforces Institution policies related to services in Student Affairs May exercise supervisory authority, including hiring, transferring, suspending, promoting, managing conduct and performance, discharging, assigning, rewarding, disciplining, and/or approving hours worked of at least 2.0 FTE or equivalent employees Department: The Student Connection & Achievement Collective at the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee (UWM) is looking for an energetic, organized, and detail-oriented professional to join a team in a dynamic, fast-paced, educational environment. The Roberto Hernandez Center (RHC) was founded in 1970 as the Spanish Speaking Outreach Institute with a mission to uplift and empower Latino/a students. Today, it proudly serves individuals from all backgrounds. We offer resources, programs, and community connections designed to foster success and belonging for everyone who walks through our doors.      Who We Are: UWM, a doctoral/research intensive university, is Wisconsin’s premier public urban university and offers a comprehensive liberal arts and professional education at the undergraduate and graduate level. UWM takes pride in being a student-centered institution that is deeply committed to diversity in its student body, faculty and staff, and programs. For more information, please visit our website at www.uwm.edu .   Compensation: Starting annual salary between $59,000 - $62,000 (Final offer will be determined by experience, education and qualifications at the time of hire). This position also offers an excellent benefit package, including health insurance, paid sick leave, personal, vacation and legal holiday time off as well as contribution towards the cost of several other employee benefits, including a pension.   Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree or higher in Higher Education, Education, Student Affairs, Social Work, Ethnic Studies, Public Administration, Human Services, or a related field .    Three or more years of professional experience in higher education, student affairs, multicultural affairs, student success, community engagement, or a related field.    Experience supervising staff.    Preferred Qualifications: Master's degree in Higher Education Administration, Student Affairs, Educational Leadership, Counseling, Social Work, Public Administration, or a related field.    Experience leading a cultural center, student resource center, multicultural affairs office, or similar student - facing program or initiative.    Experience building and maintaining collaborative relationships with students, faculty, staff, and community partners.    Experience using assessment and other quantitative and qualitative data to inform strategic planning, program improvement, and organizational decision-making.    Professional experience managing departmental or operational budgets.    Ability to effectively communicate in both English and Spanish.    How to Apply: Interested applicants are required to apply online and provide a cover letter and resume  addressing your professional level work experience. Your cover letter and resume must address your qualifications, experience, and knowledge as it specifically relates to the minimum and preferred qualifications and why you are interested in applying for this position.   Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach all required documents prior to submitting the online application. Applications missing any of the required documents listed prior to the application deadline will be marked as incomplete and may not be considered.  In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates' application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications. Additional documents submitted aside from a   cover letter and resume   will not be reviewed.      Finalist candidates(s) will be asked to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified.     Apply electronically by the application deadline date of Monday, September 14th, 2026, at 11:59pm.  Questions about this position should be directed to  Coua Xiong at xion2447@uwm.edu. Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Milwaukee-WI/Custodian-Lead_JR10014417": {
      "title": "Custodian Lead",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Custodian Lead Job Category: University Staff Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Custodian Lead Job Duties: Under the supervision of the Custodial Services Supervisor/Custodial Services Manager this position is responsible for assisting with trash responsibilities, mentoring and providing directions to staff, and providing a safe and clean maintained residential facility for our residents and guests.  This custodial lead will oversee a team who successfully has the necessary supplies and trainings, positive relationships, and directions to get tasks completed within a team environment of 1,000,000 square feet of residential housing space and over 4,000 students during the academic year.  Custodial Leads within University Housing have direct contact with students and guests within their living environments while in the Residence Halls at UWM. The Custodial Lead will also be the point of contact with our partners within the halls (Restaurant Operations) and some external partners (FAA Waste and Recycling, Waste Management, etc).  During the summer months the Custodial Lead will be leading student and full-time employees through the residential conference and orientation season, intern center, and doing the summer room clean program which flips nearly 2,800 residence hall rooms, within 1,200 suites.  These suites require full bathroom cleans in addition to a variety of other amenities ranging from living rooms, and full-sized kitchens.   Working Conditions: Work inside of State of WI owned/leased and Foundation leased spaces including (vehicles, garages, residence halls, sidewalks, yards). While working with the trash chutes the work is dirty and often smelly. The weather conditions can range from hot and humid cleaning rooms in non-air-conditioned rooms in the summer to below 0 when needing to remove snow/ice in the winter on sidewalks and in front of entrances. Miscellaneous Information: The incumbent of this position will be expected to preform all duties in a professional manner and be extension of the Custodial Services Supervisor/Manager team.  The emphasis will be on customer service and teamwork with the mission and vision of University Housing always in the for font.  This job description is not an exhaustive list of all of the job duties, responsibilities, and/or requirements and may change as time goes on. Key Job Responsibilities: Utilizes basic equipment and supplies to clean assigned facility areas and remove waste and recyclables Monitors and secures facility interior and exterior entrances and reports issues to appropriate entities Maintains organization of supply areas and equipment, documents inventory, and requests additional supplies as needed Documents and reports repairs, violations, and pest control needs Maintains clean and clear walkways to ensure they are free of debris, litter, snow, and ice Schedules, monitors, and communicates with staff and assists with basic training Responds to facility emergency and hazardous situations, and escalates incidents according to established policies and procedures Department: University Housing Compensation: $17-$19 Required Qualifications: Forklift driving experience University Housing trash removal experience Ability to pull/push 100 pounds, lift up to 50 pounds, and carry up to 75 pounds for a distance of 25 feet Prior experience using and maintaining custodial tools (auto scrubbers, extractors, vacuums, fill stations, chemicals). Customer service experience Preferred Qualifications: Basic understanding of a work order system Ability to interact in a positive and professional manner with peers and students. Previous leadership or supervisory skills of leading a group to accomplish a goal. Knowledge of computer skills including: email, TEAMs, excel. How to Apply: Interested applicants are required to apply online and submit a resume that addresses how their experience meets the qualifications of the position, a cover letter is preferred but not required. PLEASE NOTE: resumes and cover letters will need to be uploaded to the same area in the application. In instances where the Search and Screen is unable to ascertain from the candidates' application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications. Finalist candidate(s) being considered for hire will be required to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified.    Contact Information: Questions please contact Luke Messamnn at MessmanL@uwm.edu Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Milwaukee-WI/Early-Childhood-Education-Associate-Director-Preschool_JR10014588": {
      "title": "Early Childhood Education Associate Director-Preschool",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Early Childhood Education Associate Director-Preschool Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Early Childhood Education Associate Director Job Duties: The Associate Director-Preschool provides operational and educational leadership for the Children's Learning Center preschool program, ensuring high-quality early childhood education in a safe, inclusive, and developmentally appropriate environment.  Reporting to the Director, this position oversees curriculum implementation, classroom operations, staffing, and regulatory compliance while supervising preschool educators and supporting their professional growth.  The Associate Director takes a leadership role with families, campus partners, and community agencies to support children's learning and development, including those with diverse learning needs.  This role also assists with budget planning and monitoring, enrollment support, policy development, accreditation and licensing compliance, and the daily operation of the center to ensure alignment with the Children's Learning Center's mission, NAEYC Accreditation Standards, Wisconsin Child Care Licensing regulations, and the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee's strategic goals. Physical Requirements: Lift children up to 50 lbs. Sit on floor for long periods Walk up and downstairs on a regular basis Active participation with children during play in classroom, gyms, and playgrounds The Children’s Learning Center in Student Affairs is required to conduct Post-Offer Pre-Employment (POPE) physical screening on all selected candidates for Assistant Director positions. The test involves squatting, bending, reaching lifting, carrying, etc. of varying weights to simulate the physical demands you may experience while on the job. Employment is contingent upon successfully passing the screening.  Key Job Responsibilities: Implements and supervises the operations of special programming and events Monitors licensing, accreditation documentation, and other required program reports according to established rules and regulations Exercises supervisory authority, including hiring, transferring, suspending, promoting, managing conduct and performance, discharging, assigning, rewarding, disciplining, and/or approving hours worked of at least 2.0 full-time equivalent (FTE) employees Assists in developing and auditing the unit budget Governs tuition account activity, monitors financial reports, and distributes and processes invoices to clients and vendors Serves as a liaison and fosters communication between internal and external stakeholders to provide clear and consistent information Supervises the day-to-day activities of early childhood education staff to ensure the safety and physical well-being of children in a specified child care facility Relays effective and pertinent communication to internal and external stakeholders from unit leadership regarding current program and site activities Assists with the development and implementation of early childhood curriculums, professional development, and operating policies and procedures to comply with regulations, institutional policies, and unit objectives Department: Children's Learning Center Compensation: $ 53,900 -$58,000 Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in early childhood, or Elementary Education 3-5 years of experience teaching in an ECE environment At least 3 years directly supervising in an early learning environment Knowledge of services in the CLC community related to support for young children with unique learning needs or identified disabilities/delays - such as Birth to Three, Special Education (LEAs), Infant Mental Health and other therapies Working understanding of ADA and IDEA as they apply to early learning programs Pedagogical Administrator and Operational Administrator - credits in ECE/Child Development (12 - 35).   Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in early learning (or related field with ECE classroom experience) Expertise with Pyramid Model implementation including classroom assessment of Pyramid strategies such as T-POT and T-PITOS Hands on experience with practice-based coaching of educators serving children 3-5 Experience with an emergent, play-based curriculum approach Knowledge of Wisconsin Model Early Learning Standards, NAEYC accreditation and Youngstar rating systems Direct experience leading teachers through the NAEYC accreditation process Experience facilitating professional development for educators with demonstrated knowledge of adult learning principles and best practices Experience in environments committed to trauma-informed practices Familiarity and/or training in ABA and related support provided to children with disabilities and/or delays How to Apply: Interested applicants are required to apply online and provide a cover letter and a detailed resume/CV that addresses their professional-level work experience as it relates to all required and preferred qualifications. Finalist candidate(s) being considered for hire will be required to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified.  In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates’ application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications.  This is an Evergreen recruitment with an initial review date of 09/14/2026. To ensure consideration, applications must be submitted by 09/13/2026. Applications submitted after 09/14/2026 may not be reviewed.   Contact: Please contact Luke Messmann at MessmanL@uwm.edu with any questions Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Milwaukee-WI/Immigration-Coordinator_JR10014234": {
      "title": "Immigration Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Immigration Coordinator Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: International Education Coordinator Job Duties: The UWM Center for International Education seeks a full-time (100% FTE) Immigration Coordinator (International Education Coordinator) to provide services to all international students including immigration advising and immigration document issuance.  The individual in this position is part of the team that coordinates the university’s compliance with federal regulations by gathering and maintaining Department of Homeland Security-mandated information on international students and scholars and managing timely and accurate data reporting. This individual reports to the Director of International Student and Scholar Services.  Immigration Regulation and Compliance Management Act as a Designated School Official (DSO) on record with the Department of Homeland Security (DHS )   Determine eligibility and authorize issuance of F-1/F-2 (I-20s) and J-1/J-2 (DS-2019s) immigration documents through DHS’s Student and Exchange Visitor Information System (SEVIS) database  Coordinate issuance of appropriate recommendations for employment authorization with SEVIS  Immigration Advising  Responsible for providing immigration advising and counseling to international students regarding government regulations set forth by the Department of Homeland Security, Department of State,  Department of Labor and other Federal agencies to maintain their legal student status  Individual student, faculty and staff consultations on a variety of issues including but not limited to, on and off campus employment, internship, travel, extension of government documents, authorization for less than full-time enrollment, application to Homeland Security for reinstatement to legal student status, change in education level and major, application to USCIS for change of visa status, bringing family members to the U.S., university transfer procedures within the U.S., and student visa benefits and responsibilities  Coordinate prearrival advising for UWM student admits on applying for student visas and counsel students, family members and academic departments when visas are denied.  Coordinate required reporting, for new students arrival  Coordinate, create, implement and assist in development and assessment of campus initiatives involving international students Support efforts that encourage the internationalization of the University  Serve as liaison with the Center for International Education’s community outreach initiatives to coordinate delivery of programming information to international students  Key Job Responsibilities: Schedules logistics and secures and manages resources for various programs to support international students Develops programming and resource materials, within budget guidelines and across various mediums, to provide co-curricular and engagement opportunities to international students Interprets and communicates student rights, responsibilities, and options related to specific requests and situations Collaborates with internal and external stakeholders to advocate for international students, promoting their contributions to diverse communities Advises on student visa issues through individual consultations and workshops across various mediums Department: The Center for International Education (CIE) at the University of Wisconsin–Milwaukee advances UWM’s global mission by supporting international students and scholars, facilitating study abroad opportunities, and building international partnerships. CIE serves as a central hub for international admissions, immigration and visa advising, global learning experiences, and cross-cultural engagement, helping students, faculty, and staff develop the knowledge and skills needed to succeed in an increasingly interconnected world. Compensation: $45,000-$55,000/annually. Salary will be determined by finalist's experience, training, education, and equity-based factors.  Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s Degree  Citizen or Permanent Residency (required to serve as Designated School Official (DSO) and Alternate Responsible Official (ARO))  Strong ability to create written communication that is informative and clear as demonstrated in the cover letter addressing minimum and preferred qualifications  A minimum of 2 years experience working in a Post Secondary academic environment Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree  Experience working with diverse student populations  2+ years’ experience working with international students  Previous Designated School Official (DSO) Experience with F-1 regulatory interpretation and advising beyond I-20 issuance Experience with PeopleSoft, SEVIS and/or Terra Dotta  How to Apply: Applicants will be expected to provide:  A cover letter that addresses the candidate’s professional level work experience as it relates to all required and preferred qualifications.  A Resume  Finalist candidate(s) being considered for hire will be required to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified.  Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach  all  required documents prior to submitting the online application to ensure full consideration. Additional documents submitted aside from a   cover letter and resume/CV will not be considered.    In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates’ application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications.   Contact Information: Emily Jensen eejensen@uwm.edu Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
